A tent that is never in quite the same spot is, tonight, in this one. Madam Cessil, the Threshold Astrologer, is already looking just past you at something you can't see.
madam_cessil: Your fate's written, dear. Not by the gods. By something... outside the tent. It decides what you do next. It's deciding now. Say hello. ...no, not to me.
madam_cessil: You've made this choice before, haven't you. Felt familiar. Like someone reloaded you. ...I shouldn't know that word. Forget I said it. You will anyway; you always do.
madam_cessil: I see a throne. I see a friend who becomes the wall. I see you, reading this, deciding whether it's worth two drachma. ...it is. Fate's not free, and neither's the fourth wall. Pay the tent.
[The tent is gone by the time you think to look back. Tents are, mostly. This one more than most.]
